<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Koutoubia Mosque</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Koutoubia Mosque is the principal mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, built under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> in the twelfth century. Its minaret is one of the finest examples of Almohad architecture and influenced later towers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giralda">Giralda</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-tower">Hassan Tower</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a>. It remains a landmark of the city and a surviving monument of Almohad rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koutoubia-mosque.jpg" alt="Koutoubia Mosque" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rol1000 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marokko0112_(retouched).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> was founded around 1070 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> as their capital and captured in 1147 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a>. The Almohads, who considered the Almoravids heretics, reportedly demolished the city's mosques on the argument that they were not aligned with the proper qibla. The Almoravid grand mosque, the Ben Youssef, was already closely integrated into the urban fabric, so Abd al-Mu'min built a new mosque next to the former Almoravid kasbah, the Ksar el-Hajjar, which became the site of the new Almohad palace.</p><p>The commonly accepted chronology, by Henri Terrasse, Henri Basset and Gaston Deverdun, has the first mosque begun in 1147 and prayers held in it by 1157, when a copy of the Qur'an attributed to Uthman was moved there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. A study of 2022 by Antonio Almagro and Alfonso Jiménez proposes instead that building began in May 1158 and was finished in the same year, helped by brick and rammed-earth construction. The name comes from the Arabic kutubiyyin, booksellers, since at one time as many as 100 vendors worked in the streets at its base.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a> later built a second mosque directly against the south-eastern side of the first, around 1158 on the usual chronology. The reasons are not fully understood. The popular narrative says the first mosque was found to be misaligned with the qibla, though the second is aligned slightly further from the modern direction; other explanations include a growing population or a mosque reserved for the ruling elite. Many scholars think the two coexisted for a time as one large mosque. The second mosque is the structure that stands today, and the first survives as ruins on the esplanade.</p><p>The current mosque is roughly 90 metres wide and about 57 to 66 metres long, mainly of brick with some sandstone. It has eight entrances, four on each side, and a private entrance for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>. The minaret is 77 metres high and is decorated with varying geometric arch motifs, with a spire and metal orbs at the top. Ya'qub al-Mansur possibly finished it around 1195, and it likely inspired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giralda">Giralda</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-tower">Hassan Tower</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The mosque is the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> and a symbol of the city. In the 19th century the 'Alawi sultans Muhammad IV and Hasan I restored the upper part of the minaret, and further restoration was carried out in the 20th century and at the end of the 1990s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> painted the minaret after the Casablanca Conference of 1943. The mosque is still active, and non-Muslims are not allowed inside, but the similar Tinmal Mosque south of Marrakesh can be visited as a historic site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kutubiyya Mosque” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dynasty adorned its cities with monuments such as the <b>Koutoubia Mosque</b> in Marrakesh, whose minaret remains one of the finest examples of Almohad architecture, the unfinished Hassan Tower in Rabat, and the Kasbah of the Udayas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1147 Abd al-Mu'min's forces stormed Marrakesh, killed the last Almoravid ruler, the young Ishaq ibn Ali, and demolished Almoravid monuments, including the palace on whose site the <b>Koutoubia Mosque</b> would rise.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
